  "verbalization": "Dalfampridine is shown to bind to multiple potassium channel subunits, including Kcnc3, Kcna7, and Kcng4, all of which are involved in delayed rectifier potassium channel activity. This activity is linked to Kcnq5, a key component of the delayed rectifier potassium channel. The consistent evidence across three paths suggests a mechanistic connection where Dalfampridine modulates potassium channel function, potentially influencing Kcnq5 activity. The high scores in all paths indicate a strong, reliable interaction, supported by the shared pathway of delayed rectifier potassium channel participation. The mechanism appears to involve Dalfampridine's ability to interact with and modulate these channels, which in turn affects Kcnq5. The evidence supports a direct, biologically plausible relationship between Dalfampridine and Kcnq5, mediated through the delayed rectifier potassium channel pathway.",
